00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:21.399
Part 1.

19
00:01:21.679 --> 00:01:23.599
Introduction and Why It Matters.

20
00:01:24.849 --> 00:01:27.239
A story: payment successful, no order.

21
00:01:27.519 --> 00:01:37.119
The first version of checkout was a single Spring service that called Razorpay, then inventory, then saved the order, all inside one @Transactional method.

22
00:01:37.399 --> 00:01:38.551
It looked atomic.

23
00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:46.041
It wasn't: @Transactional covered only the order database, while Razorpay and the inventory service were remote calls.

24
00:01:46.321 --> 00:01:48.796
During the monsoon sale these failures showed up:

25
00:01:49.076 --> 00:01:52.980
Razorpay captured the payment, then the inventory call timed out.

26
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:58.529
The method threw, the order insert rolled back, and the customer was charged with no order.

27
00:01:58.809 --> 00:02:03.012
1,140 customers had to be refunded by hand.

28
00:02:04.362 --> 00:02:08.821
Inventory decremented stock, then the service crashed before saving the order.

29
00:02:09.101 --> 00:02:17.399
The stock stayed decremented forever, so the app showed sold out while 380 units of Alphonso mangoes were sitting in the dark stores.

30
00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:24.783
The order was saved and the order placed Kafka event was sent after the commit, but the broker was briefly unavailable.

31
00:02:25.063 --> 00:02:28.413
The picking service never heard about 212 orders.

32
00:02:28.693 --> 00:02:30.933
They sat in Placed until customers called.

33
00:02:31.213 --> 00:02:34.242
Retries after timeouts charged some customers twice.

34
00:02:34.522 --> 00:02:37.445
The payment had succeeded, but the response was lost.

35
00:02:38.795 --> 00:02:44.704
The team first tried distributed transactions (XA or 2PC) across the services.

36
00:02:44.984 --> 00:02:46.968
They dropped the idea after a pilot.

37
00:02:47.248 --> 00:02:55.973
A coordinator restart left inventory rows locked for 11 minutes while it recovered, which blocked every other checkout for those SKUs.

38
00:02:56.253 --> 00:02:58.984
Razorpay doesn't take part in XA anyway.

39
00:02:59.264 --> 00:03:01.227
The design that worked is this chapter:

40
00:03:01.507 --> 00:03:03.107
Each service commits locally.

41
00:03:03.387 --> 00:03:13.264
The order row and the order placed event are written in one local transaction (the transactional outbox), and a relay publishes the event later, at least once.

42
00:03:14.614 --> 00:03:23.830
Consumers are idempotent (an inbox or dedup table in the same transaction as the effect), so at least once becomes exactly once in effect.

43
00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:33.966
Checkout is a saga: a sequence of local transactions (reserve stock, capture payment, confirm), each with a compensation (release, refund, cancel).

44
00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:38.598
An orchestrator persists its state, so a crash resumes instead of forgetting.

45
00:03:38.878 --> 00:03:45.982
Every remote step has an idempotency key (the saga id), so retrying after a timeout never charges twice.

46
00:03:47.332 --> 00:03:54.244
Stock is held with an expiry (try-confirm-cancel), so an abandoned checkout gives the mango back automatically.

47
00:03:55.594 --> 00:03:56.277
Quote:

48
00:03:56.557 --> 00:03:58.221
The thesis of this chapter.

49
00:03:58.501 --> 00:04:01.402
Across services, you can't have atomicity for free.

50
00:04:01.682 --> 00:04:12.946
You can have atomic local steps, reliable messaging between them, idempotent handlers, and explicit compensations, which gives eventual consistency with invariants you can check.

51
00:04:13.226 --> 00:04:20.757
Use 2PC only inside one trust and failure domain, where you control every participant and can tolerate blocking.

52
00:04:22.107 --> 00:04:24.411
