107
00:06:48.525 --> 00:06:50.402
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

108
00:06:51.652 --> 00:06:54.660
Intuition: booking a wedding in three different shops.

109
00:06:54.940 --> 00:07:00.913
You're organizing a wedding: the hall, the caterer and the photographer, three independent businesses.

110
00:07:01.193 --> 00:07:03.220
No single undo button exists.

111
00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.508
You can't make all three bookings in one atomic step.

112
00:07:06.788 --> 00:07:08.345
You book each one separately.

113
00:07:08.625 --> 00:07:14.108
If the photographer is unavailable after you've booked the hall and the caterer, you cancel those two.

114
00:07:14.388 --> 00:07:21.919
Cancellation isn't as if it never happened: there may be a cancellation fee, and the hall may have turned someone else away meanwhile.

115
00:07:22.199 --> 00:07:24.140
That's a saga with compensations.

116
00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:28.583
You keep a notebook of what you've booked and what's left to do.

117
00:07:28.863 --> 00:07:32.831
If you fall ill halfway, your sibling reads the notebook and continues.

118
00:07:33.111 --> 00:07:35.202
That's the orchestrator's persisted state.

119
00:07:35.482 --> 00:07:44.378
If the phone line drops after you asked the caterer to book, you call back and say this is booking W-42, did it go through? rather than booking again.

120
00:07:44.658 --> 00:07:46.215
That's an idempotency key.

121
00:07:46.495 --> 00:07:49.610
The hall holds your date for 48 hours without payment.

122
00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.023
If you don't confirm, the hold lapses.

123
00:07:52.303 --> 00:07:55.119
That's a reservation with expiry (TCC).

124
00:07:56.469 --> 00:08:06.347
Two-phase commit would be asking all three shops to put a pending sticker on your date and refuse everyone else until you personally come back and say confirm or cancel.

125
00:08:06.627 --> 00:08:10.061
If you're stuck in traffic, three businesses stop selling that date.

126
00:08:11.411 --> 00:08:14.782
Why local transactions don't extend across services.

127
00:08:15.062 --> 00:08:18.582
A database transaction gives ACID within that database.

128
00:08:18.862 --> 00:08:29.443
In a microservice architecture (Ch 16), each service owns its data, so one business operation touches several databases plus external APIs:

129
00:08:30.793 --> 00:08:32.308
