130
00:08:35.158 --> 00:08:38.465
Every failure in §1.1 comes from this shape.

131
00:08:38.745 --> 00:08:40.025
What you want instead:

132
00:08:40.305 --> 00:08:44.273
Each step is a local transaction in the service that owns the data.

133
00:08:44.553 --> 00:08:47.774
Steps are connected by reliable messages or calls.

134
00:08:48.054 --> 00:08:49.569
Each step is idempotent.

135
00:08:49.849 --> 00:08:51.769
Failures trigger compensations.

136
00:08:53.119 --> 00:08:54.484
The dual-write problem.

137
00:08:54.764 --> 00:09:05.708
Any time a service must update its database and notify another system (a broker, a cache, a search index, another service), there are two writes and no transaction spanning them.

138
00:09:05.988 --> 00:09:09.892
L1 counted what goes wrong over 100,000 orders:

139
00:09:11.242 --> 00:09:14.997
On screen: strategy lost events phantom events duplicates.

140
00:09:15.277 --> 00:09:19.309
DBTHENPUBLISH 1696 0 0.

141
00:09:19.589 --> 00:09:23.301
PUBLISHTHENDB 0 1118 0.

142
00:09:23.581 --> 00:09:26.461
OUTBOX 0 0 1017.

143
00:09:29.311 --> 00:09:34.303
Commit, then publish: a crash or broker outage after the commit loses the event.

144
00:09:34.583 --> 00:09:37.079
The order exists, and nobody hears about it.

145
00:09:37.359 --> 00:09:44.484
Publish, then commit: a crash or rollback after publishing creates a phantom event for an order that doesn't exist.

146
00:09:44.764 --> 00:09:50.481
Outbox: write the event into an outbox table in the same local transaction as the order.

147
00:09:50.761 --> 00:09:52.532
A separate relay publishes it.

148
00:09:52.812 --> 00:09:59.852
Nothing is lost and nothing is phantom, and the only failure mode left is duplicates, which consumers can remove.

149
00:10:01.202 --> 00:10:12.274
Publish in afterCommit (Spring's @TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTERCOMMIT)) is still commit-then-publish: a crash between commit and send loses the event.

