Both are at least once: a relay that crashes after publishing but before marking publishes again (L2:

187
00:11:53.967 --> 00:12:01.540
13 to 47 relay crashes and 246 to 488 duplicate deliveries per run).

188
00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:08.817
Ordering: a consumer usually needs order per aggregate (placed before confirmed for the same order), not globally.

189
00:12:09.097 --> 00:12:16.500
Use one relay per partition, or FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED with a single active relay (leader-elected, Ch 12).

190
00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:23.500
Publish with the aggregate id as the Kafka key, so all its events go to one partition (Ch 14).

191
00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:33.469
Carry a per-aggregate seq, so consumers can detect and handle out-of-order or stale events (Ch 12 L2's versioned status).

192
00:12:33.749 --> 00:12:35.370
Outbox ids have gaps.

193
00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:42.114
The lab's rolled-back transaction consumed id 2, and the CDC stream showed ids 1 and 3.

194
00:12:42.394 --> 00:12:45.679
Concurrent transactions also commit out of id order.

195
00:12:45.959 --> 00:12:50.333
Never infer all events up to N have been published from id continuity.

196
00:12:51.683 --> 00:12:54.008
The inbox: idempotent consumers.

197
00:12:54.288 --> 00:13:06.299
At-least-once delivery means every consumer sees duplicates: relay retries, broker redelivery after a rebalance, a consumer crash after the effect but before the offset commit.

198
00:13:06.579 --> 00:13:12.189
The standard fix is to record the message id in the same local transaction as the effect:

199
00:13:13.539 --> 00:13:17.465
Here is the SQL code. ... apply the effect (reserve stock)...

200
00:13:20.315 --> 00:13:23.429
L2, 2,000 orders with injected crashes:

201
00:13:23.709 --> 00:13:24.904
Without an inbox:

202
00:13:25.184 --> 00:13:32.907
2,403 reservation rows for 2,000 orders, so 403 units of stock were taken twice.

203
00:13:33.187 --> 00:13:39.928
With an inbox: exactly 2,000, even with 3 relays and 488 duplicate deliveries.

204
00:13:40.208 --> 00:13:51.813
Alternatives are natural idempotency (insert... on conflict do nothing keyed by orderid, update... where version:v), or idempotency keys at an API (Ch 20).

205
00:13:52.093 --> 00:13:59.816
Keep inbox rows at least as long as the broker can redeliver (retention + replay window), then delete in batches.

206
00:14:01.166 --> 00:14:07.481
Exactly once in Kafka (idempotent producer + transactions) covers Kafka-to-Kafka processing.

207
00:14:07.761 --> 00:14:15.761
The moment the effect is in a database or an external API, you still need idempotent handling at that boundary (Ch 14).

208
00:14:17.111 --> 00:14:19.735
Two-phase commit (2PC) and XA, then.

209
00:14:20.015 --> 00:14:23.791
2PC makes several resource managers commit atomically:

210
00:14:24.071 --> 00:14:28.167
Prepare (voting): the coordinator asks each participant to prepare.

211
00:14:28.447 --> 00:14:34.996
A participant that votes yes must guarantee it can commit later: it persists its changes and keeps its locks.

212
00:14:35.276 --> 00:14:42.167
Commit or abort (decision): if all voted yes, the coordinator logs the decision and tells everyone to commit.

213
00:14:42.447 --> 00:14:44.559
Otherwise it tells everyone to abort.

214
00:14:44.839 --> 00:14:50.535
Why 2PC hurts (all shown with PostgreSQL's PREPARE TRANSACTION in lab 1):

215
00:14:51.885 --> 00:14:55.725
Blocking: after voting yes, a participant can't decide alone.

216
00:14:56.005 --> 00:15:00.549
If the coordinator dies, the transaction is in doubt and its locks stay held.

217
00:15:00.829 --> 00:15:07.101
In the lab another checkout for the same SKU failed with canceling statement due to lock timeout.

218
00:15:07.381 --> 00:15:20.223
Prepared transactions survive restarts: after restarting the database server, ord-1003:inv was still in doubt and the row was still locked, until the coordinator came back and ran COMMIT PREPARED.

219
00:15:20.503 --> 00:15:26.989
Latency: at least two round trips plus forced log writes per participant, and locks are held across all of it.

220
00:15:28.339 --> 00:15:33.992
Availability: the transaction commits only if every participant and the coordinator are up.

221
00:15:34.272 --> 00:15:37.643
With availability a each, n participants give roughly aⁿ.

222
00:15:37.923 --> 00:15:46.520
Participation: every participant must support XA (Razorpay, Kafka's own transactions, Redis, most SaaS APIs don't).

223
00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:51.920
Heuristic decisions (an admin forcing a commit or rollback) can break atomicity.

224
00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:58.408
Reads aren't blocked (MVCC showed the old stock value), but writers queue behind the in-doubt locks.

225
00:15:59.758 --> 00:16:09.038
3PC adds a pre-commit phase so participants can make progress when the coordinator fails, but only in a synchronous network with bounded delays.

226
00:16:09.318 --> 00:16:12.262
It isn't safe under partitions, so it's rarely used.

227
00:16:12.542 --> 00:16:30.441
Consensus-based commit (Spanner and CockroachDB run 2PC where each participant is itself a Raft or Paxos group, and the coordinator state is replicated) removes the coordinator dies blocking, which is why distributed SQL can offer multi-shard ACID.

228
00:16:31.791 --> 00:16:39.940
When 2PC is acceptable: inside one database product across its own shards, which is what distributed SQL does.

229
00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:49.479
Or XA between a database and a message queue in the same data centre where you accept the blocking risk (the classic JMS + DB setup).

230
00:16:49.759 --> 00:16:53.001
Not across microservices or external APIs.

231
00:16:54.351 --> 00:16:55.247
Sagas.

232
00:16:55.527 --> 00:17:02.951
A saga (Garcia-Molina and Salem, 1987) is a sequence of local transactions T1...Tn.

233
00:17:03.231 --> 00:17:11.231
Each Ti publishes an event or triggers the next step, and each has a compensating transaction Ci that semantically undoes it.

234
00:17:11.511 --> 00:17:14.413
If Tk fails, run Ck-1...C1.

235
00:17:14.693 --> 00:17:15.781
Types of steps:

236
00:17:16.061 --> 00:17:20.733
Compensatable: they can be undone (reserve stock ↔ release).

237
00:17:21.013 --> 00:17:23.039
Pivot: the go or no-go point.

238
00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:29.250
After it, the saga must complete (payment capture: once captured, you'd rather deliver than refund).

239
00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:39.219
Retriable: after the pivot, steps must eventually succeed, retried with idempotency (confirm reservation, send the confirmation).

240
00:17:39.499 --> 00:17:44.192
Put the steps that are most likely to fail first, and the pivot as late as possible.

241
00:17:44.472 --> 00:17:46.797
Orchestration versus choreography:

242
00:17:48.147 --> 00:17:49.491
Here is a quick table:

243
00:17:49.771 --> 00:17:51.883
Orchestration, and Choreography.

244
00:17:52.163 --> 00:17:53.017
Control.

245
00:17:53.297 --> 00:17:54.321
Orchestration:

246
00:17:54.601 --> 00:17:59.315
A central orchestrator (a state machine) tells each participant what to do.

247
00:17:59.595 --> 00:18:00.683
Choreography:

248
00:18:00.963 --> 00:18:03.630
Each service reacts to events and emits its own.

249
00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:04.849
Visibility.

250
00:18:05.129 --> 00:18:06.153
Orchestration:

251
00:18:06.433 --> 00:18:09.185
The whole flow in one place and one state table.

252
00:18:09.465 --> 00:18:10.553
Choreography:

253
00:18:10.833 --> 00:18:14.395
Implicit, spread across services, so you need tracing to see it.

254
00:18:14.675 --> 00:18:15.443
Coupling.

255
00:18:15.723 --> 00:18:16.747
Orchestration:

256
00:18:17.027 --> 00:18:18.926
The orchestrator knows every step.

257
00:18:19.206 --> 00:18:20.294
Choreography:

258
00:18:20.574 --> 00:18:22.899
Services know the events, not each other.

259
00:18:23.179 --> 00:18:25.803
Cyclic dependencies / event spaghetti.

260
00:18:26.083 --> 00:18:27.107
Orchestration:

261
00:18:27.387 --> 00:18:28.262
Avoided.

262
00:18:28.542 --> 00:18:29.630
Choreography:

263
00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:31.403
A risk as flows grow.

264
00:18:32.753 --> 00:18:33.585
Good for.

265
00:18:33.865 --> 00:18:34.889
Orchestration:

266
00:18:35.169 --> 00:18:40.161
Checkout, refunds, onboarding: flows with ordering, compensations and timeouts.

267
00:18:40.441 --> 00:18:41.529
Choreography:

268
00:18:41.809 --> 00:18:45.607
Simple fan-out: notify picking, analytics, loyalty points.

269
00:18:45.887 --> 00:18:46.761
Tools.

270
00:18:47.041 --> 00:18:48.065
Orchestration:

271
00:18:48.345 --> 00:18:54.191
Hand-rolled state machine (L3), Temporal, Camunda or Zeebe, AWS Step Functions.

272
00:18:54.471 --> 00:18:55.559
Choreography:

273
00:18:55.839 --> 00:18:57.951
Kafka + outbox + inbox.

274
00:18:59.301 --> 00:19:01.562
Orchestrator rules (all in L3):

275
00:19:01.842 --> 00:19:07.517
Persist saga state before and after each step, keyed by saga id, so a crash resumes.

276
00:19:07.797 --> 00:19:13.727
L3 crashed the orchestrator 310 times, and a recovery loop resumed every saga.

277
00:19:14.007 --> 00:19:18.210
Every step and compensation is idempotent, keyed by saga id.

278
00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:22.885
A crash after the effect but before saving the state means the step runs again.

279
00:19:24.235 --> 00:19:27.349
Handle unknown outcomes: a timeout means maybe.

280
00:19:27.629 --> 00:19:31.533
Retry with the same idempotency key to learn the real result.

281
00:19:31.813 --> 00:19:42.821
Never treat a timeout as failure and compensate blindly (you'd refund a charge the gateway says doesn't exist, or release stock for a confirmed order), and never retry without a key.

282
00:19:43.101 --> 00:19:48.264
Compensate in reverse order, and make compensations retriable until they succeed.

283
00:19:48.544 --> 00:19:52.277
A compensation that can't fail still needs retries (the network).

284
00:19:52.557 --> 00:20:04.781
State transitions are conditional (update saga set state =:to where id =:id and state =:from), so two orchestrator instances can't both advance the same saga.

285
00:20:06.131 --> 00:20:11.614
L3's negative control made steps non-idempotent with everything else unchanged.

286
00:20:11.894 --> 00:20:12.705
Result:

287
00:20:12.985 --> 00:20:18.745
141 double-charged orders and 247 invariant violations.

288
00:20:19.025 --> 00:20:20.390
With idempotent steps:

289
00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:21.865
0 and 0.

290
00:20:23.215 --> 00:20:26.073
Sagas lack isolation, and what to do about it.

291
00:20:26.353 --> 00:20:31.964
A saga's intermediate states are visible to other transactions: it's ACD without the I.

292
00:20:32.244 --> 00:20:33.140
Anomalies:

293
00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:38.284
Dirty reads: another saga sees stock held by a saga that later compensates.

294
00:20:38.564 --> 00:20:48.953
L3 turned away 115 checkouts as OUTOFSTOCK, yet 21 units were left at the end: they had been held by sagas that later failed payment and released them.

295
00:20:49.233 --> 00:20:58.513
Lost updates: a compensation overwrites a change another saga made meanwhile (restoring a previous value instead of applying an inverse delta).

296
00:20:59.863 --> 00:21:06.263
Fuzzy or non-repeatable reads: a step reads data that another saga changes before a later step.

297
00:21:06.543 --> 00:21:11.066
Countermeasures (Garcia-Molina; Richardson's Microservices Patterns):

298
00:21:12.416 --> 00:21:13.760
Here is a quick table:

299
00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:17.133
Countermeasure, and Idea, and In L3 or L4.

300
00:21:17.413 --> 00:21:18.544
Semantic lock.

301
00:21:18.824 --> 00:21:19.613
Idea:

302
00:21:19.893 --> 00:21:25.504
Mark records as pending (status = HELD or PENDING) so others know they're in flux.

303
00:21:25.784 --> 00:21:27.277
In L3 or L4:

304
00:21:27.557 --> 00:21:29.883
Reservations HELD, orders PENDING.

305
00:21:30.163 --> 00:21:31.464
Commutative updates.

306
00:21:31.744 --> 00:21:32.533
Idea:

307
00:21:32.813 --> 00:21:41.219
Design operations so order doesn't matter: qty = qty - 1 / + 1, not set qty = 7.

308
00:21:41.499 --> 00:21:42.992
In L3 or L4:

309
00:21:43.272 --> 00:21:45.149
Stock decrements or increments.

310
00:21:45.429 --> 00:21:46.624
Pessimistic view.

311
00:21:46.904 --> 00:21:47.693
Idea:

312
00:21:47.973 --> 00:21:52.496
Reorder steps to reduce the damage of dirty reads (reserve before charging).

313
00:21:52.776 --> 00:21:54.269
In L3 or L4:

314
00:21:54.549 --> 00:21:56.235
Reserve first, pay second.

315
00:21:56.515 --> 00:21:57.581
Reread value.

316
00:21:57.861 --> 00:21:58.651
Idea:

317
00:21:58.931 --> 00:22:03.005
Re-check before overwriting (optimistic concurrency with versions).

318
00:22:03.285 --> 00:22:04.779
In L3 or L4:

319
00:22:05.059 --> 00:22:07.085
Conditional where state =:from.

320
00:22:07.365 --> 00:22:09.179
Version file or by value.

321
00:22:09.459 --> 00:22:10.248
Idea:

322
00:22:10.528 --> 00:22:15.285
Record operations and reorder them, or choose the mechanism per request risk.

323
00:22:16.635 --> 00:22:25.830
Accept the business consequence explicitly: a customer may see 'sold out' while units are held by pending checkouts; holds expire after 5 minutes.

324
00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:27.497
That's a product decision.

325
00:22:28.847 --> 00:22:31.023
TCC: try-confirm-cancel.

326
00:22:31.303 --> 00:22:36.401
TCC is a saga variant where each participant exposes three operations:

327
00:22:36.681 --> 00:22:42.463
Try: reserve resources and check business rules, without making the change final (a stock hold).

328
00:22:42.743 --> 00:22:44.236
Confirm: make it final.

329
00:22:44.516 --> 00:22:48.932
It uses only the reserved resources, so it can't fail for business reasons.

330
00:22:49.212 --> 00:22:51.111
Cancel: release the reservation.

331
00:22:51.391 --> 00:23:01.951
The key addition is expiry: a hold that is neither confirmed nor cancelled (the orchestrator died, the customer walked away) is released automatically after a TTL.

332
00:23:03.301 --> 00:23:09.829
Confirm and expiry then race, and both must be conditional updates in the database so exactly one wins.

333
00:23:10.109 --> 00:23:13.415
L4, 500 mangoes and 2,000 buyers:

334
00:23:13.695 --> 00:23:26.389
Unguarded confirm (update... where id =?) confirmed 801 units: the sweeper released 301 expired holds that were also confirmed, oversold by 301.

335
00:23:26.669 --> 00:23:33.346
Guarded confirm (where status = 'HELD' and expiresat clocktimestamp()) confirmed exactly 500.

336
00:23:33.626 --> 00:23:42.437
The 342 confirms that arrived after expiry were refused and refunded, and available + confirmed = 500.

337
00:23:43.787 --> 00:23:50.784
Expiry uses the database's clock, in the same statement (Ch 12: never compare two machines' clocks).

338
00:23:51.064 --> 00:23:59.107
Choose the TTL from the payment-time distribution (p99 + margin), and refund confirms that arrive too late.

339
00:24:00.457 --> 00:24:02.590
Change data capture (CDC).

340
00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:08.673
CDC reads a database's own change log and turns committed changes into events:

341
00:24:08.953 --> 00:24:16.057
PostgreSQL: logical decoding from a replication slot (pgoutput, testdecoding, wal2json).

342
00:24:16.337 --> 00:24:18.086
MySQL: the binlog.

343
00:24:18.366 --> 00:24:20.350
MongoDB: change streams.

344
00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:29.249
Debezium packages these as Kafka Connect source connectors, and its Outbox Event Router turns outbox inserts into topic messages.

345
00:24:30.599 --> 00:24:32.732
Properties, all visible in lab 2:

346
00:24:33.012 --> 00:24:36.895
Only committed changes, in commit order, grouped by transaction.

347
00:24:37.175 --> 00:24:39.436
The rolled-back ord-2 never appeared.

348
00:24:39.716 --> 00:24:43.535
No dual write: the application writes only to its database.

349
00:24:43.815 --> 00:24:48.209
Replication slots retain WAL until the consumer confirms it.

350
00:24:48.489 --> 00:24:56.169
A stalled connector made the primary keep 37 MB in the lab, and it can grow until the disk is full (Ch 10).

351
00:24:56.449 --> 00:24:59.905
Set maxslotwalkeepsize, monitor slot lag, and alert.

352
00:25:01.255 --> 00:25:08.210
Idle databases need a heartbeat: the slot's restart position only advances when there's something to decode.

353
00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:14.079
The lab had to emit a message before retained WAL dropped to 240 bytes.

354
00:25:14.359 --> 00:25:19.778
That's what Debezium's heartbeat.interval.ms or heartbeat.action.query are for.

355
00:25:20.058 --> 00:25:26.586
Failover: slots aren't replicated to standbys before PostgreSQL 17 (failover slots).

356
00:25:26.866 --> 00:25:30.770
After a failover, the connector may need a re-snapshot or has a gap.

357
00:25:31.050 --> 00:25:32.671
Plan for it (Ch 10).

358
00:25:34.021 --> 00:25:39.248
Schema changes flow through the log, so consumers must handle them (Ch 28).

359
00:25:39.528 --> 00:25:48.701
Emitting outbox events with an explicit schema is safer than exposing raw table changes (don't leak your internal schema as your public contract).

360
00:25:50.051 --> 00:25:51.331
Choosing an approach.

361
00:25:51.611 --> 00:25:52.955
Here is a quick table:

362
00:25:53.235 --> 00:25:54.686
Situation, and Choose.

363
00:25:54.966 --> 00:25:56.865
All data in one database:

364
00:25:57.145 --> 00:25:59.619
A local ACID transaction, nothing else.

365
00:25:59.899 --> 00:26:03.249
Can the data be co-located (same shard or database)?:

366
00:26:03.529 --> 00:26:07.006
Co-locate (Ch 11), and use a local transaction.

367
00:26:07.286 --> 00:26:10.422
One service's DB change must notify others:

368
00:26:10.702 --> 00:26:15.182
Outbox (+ polling or CDC relay) + idempotent consumers.

369
00:26:15.462 --> 00:26:18.598
Multi-step business flow across services with undo:

370
00:26:18.878 --> 00:26:22.654
Orchestrated saga + idempotency + compensations.

371
00:26:22.934 --> 00:26:26.945
Resource must be held briefly during a flow (stock, seats, slots):

372
00:26:27.225 --> 00:26:29.145
TCC holds with expiry.

373
00:26:29.425 --> 00:26:30.939
Simple fan-out of facts:

374
00:26:31.219 --> 00:26:32.734
Choreography (events).

375
00:26:34.084 --> 00:26:38.692
Several resources in one DB product or one team, blocking acceptable:

376
00:26:38.972 --> 00:26:44.945
2PC or XA, or a distributed SQL database with consensus-backed 2PC.

377
00:26:45.225 --> 00:26:47.913
An external API that isn't idempotent:

378
00:26:48.193 --> 00:26:54.977
Wrap it: your own request table + reconciliation against the provider's reports (Ch 45).

379
00:26:56.327 --> 00:26:57.223
Part 3.

380
00:26:57.503 --> 00:26:59.231
Architecture and Diagrams.

381
00:27:00.481 --> 00:27:03.020
Component view: checkout across services.

382
00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:04.644
Here is the diagram.

383
00:27:08.494 --> 00:27:13.123
The orchestrator calls participants synchronously here, with idempotent APIs.

384
00:27:13.403 --> 00:27:24.475
A message-driven orchestrator (commands and replies over Kafka, each through an outbox) is the alternative when participants must stay decoupled from the orchestrator's availability.

385
00:27:24.755 --> 00:27:29.363
Either way the saga table is the single source of truth for where is this checkout.

386
00:27:30.713 --> 00:27:32.996
Sequence: happy path, checkout saga.

387
00:27:33.276 --> 00:27:36.071
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

388
00:27:36.351 --> 00:27:38.057
C to O: checkout cart.

389
00:27:38.337 --> 00:27:43.052
O to ODB: insert order PENDING and saga STARTED in one txn.

390
00:27:43.332 --> 00:27:47.791
O to I: try reserve saga-17, ATTA-5KG x1.

391
00:27:48.071 --> 00:27:48.967
I to O:

392
00:27:49.247 --> 00:27:52.169
HELD until 14:35:00.

393
00:27:52.449 --> 00:27:55.223
O to ODB: saga STARTED to RESERVED.

394
00:27:55.503 --> 00:28:02.116
O to P: capture saga-17, 32000 paise, idempotency key saga-17.

395
00:28:02.396 --> 00:28:04.252
P to R: capture with key.

396
00:28:04.532 --> 00:28:06.068
R to P: captured.

397
00:28:06.348 --> 00:28:07.287
P to O:

398
00:28:07.567 --> 00:28:08.420
CAPTURED.

399
00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:11.367
O to ODB: saga RESERVED to PAID.

400
00:28:11.647 --> 00:28:14.271
O to I: confirm hold saga-17.

401
00:28:14.551 --> 00:28:15.447
I to O:

402
00:28:15.727 --> 00:28:16.580
CONFIRMED.

403
00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:23.153
O to ODB: order CONFIRMED, saga CONFIRMED, outbox order.confirmed in one txn.

404
00:28:23.433 --> 00:28:25.311
O to C: order confirmed.

405
00:28:29.161 --> 00:28:33.449
Sequence: failure path, a payment timeout, then the orchestrator crashes.

406
00:28:33.729 --> 00:28:36.523
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

407
00:28:36.803 --> 00:28:40.025
O to P: capture saga-18 key saga-18.

408
00:28:40.305 --> 00:28:41.755
P to R: capture.

409
00:28:42.035 --> 00:28:45.363
R to P: captured, response lost on the way back.

410
00:28:45.643 --> 00:28:47.947
P to O: timeout, outcome unknown.

411
00:28:48.227 --> 00:28:51.321
O to P: capture saga-18 again, same key.

412
00:28:51.601 --> 00:28:52.539
P to O:

413
00:28:52.819 --> 00:28:54.590
CAPTURED, from the stored result.

414
00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:58.902
O2 to ODB: find sagas not updated for 30 s.

415
00:28:59.182 --> 00:29:02.169
ODB to O2: saga-18 in RESERVED.

416
00:29:02.449 --> 00:29:05.414
O2 to P: capture saga-18, same key.

417
00:29:05.694 --> 00:29:06.761
P to O2:

418
00:29:07.041 --> 00:29:08.790
CAPTURED, no second charge.

419
00:29:09.070 --> 00:29:13.209
O2 to ODB: saga RESERVED to PAID, conditional on state.

420
00:29:13.489 --> 00:29:16.262
O2 to I: confirm hold saga-18.

421
00:29:16.542 --> 00:29:17.609
I to O2:

422
00:29:17.889 --> 00:29:19.702
CONFIRMED if hold not expired.

423
00:29:19.982 --> 00:29:22.350
O2 to ODB: order CONFIRMED.

424
00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:36.291
If step 13 returns EXPIRED (the hold lapsed during the outage), the orchestrator moves to COMPENSATING: refund, then cancel with reason HOLDEXPIRED.

425
00:29:36.571 --> 00:29:38.555
That's the refund path in L4.

426
00:29:39.905 --> 00:29:41.483
State: the checkout saga.

427
00:29:41.763 --> 00:29:43.107
Here is the diagram.

428
00:29:46.957 --> 00:29:50.008
Every arrow is a conditional update of the saga row.

429
00:29:50.288 --> 00:29:53.104
Every non-terminal state has a recovery action.

430
00:29:53.384 --> 00:29:56.861
And there's no path from PAID to CANCELLED that skips the refund.

