989
01:08:09.393 --> 01:08:14.107
Caps CDC damage (the slot is invalidated instead of filling the disk).

990
01:08:15.457 --> 01:08:16.183
Cost.

991
01:08:16.463 --> 01:08:25.444
Outbox and inbox tables add write amplification: about 1 extra insert per event on the producer and 1 per event on each consumer.

992
01:08:25.724 --> 01:08:33.191
At ~1.3 K events per second that's modest, but plan for vacuum and index bloat (partition and drop).

993
01:08:33.471 --> 01:08:42.260
Sagas cost engineering time: every step needs a compensation, idempotency storage, tests for crash points, and reconciliation.

994
01:08:42.540 --> 01:08:45.612
Use them only where the flow crosses ownership boundaries.

995
01:08:45.892 --> 01:08:49.156
Co-locating data is often cheaper (Ch 11).

996
01:08:50.506 --> 01:08:59.935
Durable-execution platforms (Temporal, Camunda) cost infrastructure and learning, but pay off when there are many sagas with timers and human steps.

997
01:09:00.215 --> 01:09:03.501
Kafka Connect or Debezium is another platform to run.

998
01:09:03.781 --> 01:09:09.903
The polling relay costs only DB load, so start there and switch when latency or load demands it.

999
