1063
01:12:21.764 --> 01:12:22.639
Part 8.

1064
01:12:22.919 --> 01:12:24.263
Interview Q and A.

1065
01:12:25.513 --> 01:12:26.345
Beginner.

1066
01:12:26.625 --> 01:12:27.649
Question 1.

1067
01:12:27.929 --> 01:12:32.302
Why can't a @Transactional method make a microservice checkout atomic?

1068
01:12:32.582 --> 01:12:33.947
Thirty-second answer:

1069
01:12:34.227 --> 01:12:36.979
It only controls the local database connection.

1070
01:12:37.259 --> 01:12:44.641
Calls to other services, payment gateways and brokers happen outside that transaction and aren't rolled back when it rolls back.

1071
01:12:44.921 --> 01:12:51.065
The result is charged-without-order, stock taken without an order, or events for orders that don't exist.

1072
01:12:52.415 --> 01:12:53.417
Question 2.

1073
01:12:53.697 --> 01:12:55.340
What's the dual-write problem?

1074
01:12:55.620 --> 01:12:56.985
Thirty-second answer:

1075
01:12:57.265 --> 01:13:04.348
A service updates its database and notifies another system (a broker, cache or index) as two separate writes.

1076
01:13:04.628 --> 01:13:13.097
A crash or failure between them leaves them inconsistent: commit-then-publish loses events, and publish-then-commit creates phantom events.

1077
01:13:14.447 --> 01:13:15.493
Question 3.

1078
01:13:15.773 --> 01:13:16.839
What is a saga?

1079
01:13:17.119 --> 01:13:18.485
Thirty-second answer:

1080
01:13:18.765 --> 01:13:27.447
A long-running business transaction split into a sequence of local transactions, one per service, where each step has a compensating action.

1081
01:13:27.727 --> 01:13:32.058
If a later step fails, completed steps are compensated in reverse order.

1082
01:13:32.338 --> 01:13:35.837
It gives eventual consistency without distributed locks.

1083
01:13:37.187 --> 01:13:38.253
Question 4.

1084
01:13:38.533 --> 01:13:42.608
What does idempotent mean, and why do distributed transactions need it?

1085
01:13:42.888 --> 01:13:44.253
Thirty-second answer:

1086
01:13:44.533 --> 01:13:49.248
An operation is idempotent if doing it twice has the same effect as once.

1087
01:13:49.528 --> 01:14:00.045
Networks and crashes make retries and redeliveries unavoidable, so every step and consumer must tolerate running again, usually with a key stored alongside the effect.

1088
01:14:01.395 --> 01:14:02.398
Intermediate.

1089
01:14:02.678 --> 01:14:03.787
Question 5.

1090
01:14:04.067 --> 01:14:07.118
Explain the transactional outbox pattern end to end.

1091
01:14:07.398 --> 01:14:08.763
Thirty-second answer:

1092
01:14:09.043 --> 01:14:14.483
Write the business change and an outbox row describing the event in one local transaction.

1093
01:14:14.763 --> 01:14:24.598
A relay, by polling with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED or tailing the WAL with CDC, publishes outbox rows to the broker and marks or deletes them.

1094
01:14:24.878 --> 01:14:27.758
Delivery is at least once, so consumers dedup.

1095
01:14:29.108 --> 01:14:30.217
Question 6.

1096
01:14:30.497 --> 01:14:33.633
How do you make a Kafka consumer's effect exactly once?

1097
01:14:33.913 --> 01:14:35.279
Thirty-second answer:

1098
01:14:35.559 --> 01:14:49.212
Accept at-least-once delivery and make the effect idempotent: record the message id in an inbox table (or use a natural key) in the same database transaction as the effect, and commit the offset afterwards.

1099
01:14:49.492 --> 01:14:51.988
Redeliveries then find the id and skip.

1100
01:14:53.338 --> 01:14:54.426
Question 7.

1101
01:14:54.706 --> 01:14:58.077
Orchestration versus choreography: when do you use each?

1102
01:14:58.357 --> 01:14:59.722
Thirty-second answer:

1103
01:15:00.002 --> 01:15:10.434
Orchestration puts a state machine in charge of the steps, which gives visibility, ordering, timeouts and compensations in one place: use it for checkout and refunds.

1104
01:15:10.714 --> 01:15:21.125
Choreography has services react to each other's events, with loose coupling and no central component: use it for simple fan-out such as notifications and analytics.

1105
01:15:22.475 --> 01:15:23.499
Question 8.

1106
01:15:23.779 --> 01:15:28.771
What happens in 2PC if the coordinator fails after participants vote yes?

1107
01:15:29.051 --> 01:15:30.416
Thirty-second answer:

1108
01:15:30.696 --> 01:15:38.483
Participants are in doubt: they can't commit or abort on their own, so they keep their locks until the coordinator recovers and tells them.

1109
01:15:38.763 --> 01:15:41.451
Other transactions touching those rows wait.

1110
01:15:41.731 --> 01:15:43.757
That's 2PC's blocking problem.

1111
01:15:45.107 --> 01:15:46.174
Question 9.

1112
01:15:46.454 --> 01:15:50.251
What is CDC, and when would you use it instead of a polling relay?

1113
01:15:50.531 --> 01:15:51.897
Thirty-second answer:

1114
01:15:52.177 --> 01:16:04.379
Change data capture reads the database's own change log (PostgreSQL WAL via logical decoding, MySQL binlog) and emits committed changes in commit order.

1115
01:16:04.659 --> 01:16:12.702
Use it for low-latency outbox publishing without polling load, or to stream data to other systems without touching application code.

1116
01:16:14.052 --> 01:16:14.820
Senior.

1117
01:16:15.100 --> 01:16:16.145
Question 10.

1118
01:16:16.425 --> 01:16:19.007
Design the checkout saga for a grocery app.

1119
01:16:19.287 --> 01:16:20.652
Thirty-second answer:

1120
01:16:20.932 --> 01:16:39.001
An orchestrated saga in order-service: create the order PENDING and the saga row, reserve stock (a TCC hold with TTL), capture payment with the saga id as the idempotency key (the pivot), confirm the hold, then confirm the order and emit the event through the outbox.

1121
01:16:39.281 --> 01:16:42.204
On a decline: refund if needed, release, cancel.

1122
01:16:42.484 --> 01:16:48.649
State is persisted per transition with conditional updates, and a recovery worker resumes stuck sagas.

1123
01:16:49.999 --> 01:16:51.130
Question 11.

1124
01:16:51.410 --> 01:16:53.842
How do you handle a payment call that times out?

1125
01:16:54.122 --> 01:16:55.487
Thirty-second answer:

1126
01:16:55.767 --> 01:16:58.477
Treat it as an unknown outcome, not a failure.

1127
01:16:58.757 --> 01:17:04.666
Retry with the same idempotency key, and the provider returns the original result if it happened.

1128
01:17:04.946 --> 01:17:10.919
If it stays unknown, park the saga and let reconciliation against the provider's records decide.

1129
01:17:11.199 --> 01:17:13.802
Never compensate or retry without the key.

1130
01:17:15.152 --> 01:17:16.240
Question 12.

1131
01:17:16.520 --> 01:17:20.595
What isolation problems do sagas have, and how do you mitigate them?

1132
01:17:20.875 --> 01:17:22.240
Thirty-second answer:

1133
01:17:22.520 --> 01:17:34.488
Intermediate states are visible to others: dirty reads (seeing a hold that later compensates), lost updates (a compensation overwriting others' changes), and non-repeatable reads.

1134
01:17:34.768 --> 01:17:44.645
Mitigations are semantic locks (status flags), commutative updates, pessimistic step ordering, re-reading values with versions, and holds with expiry.

1135
01:17:45.995 --> 01:17:47.275
Question 13.

1136
01:17:47.555 --> 01:17:51.886
What's TCC, and how do you prevent overselling with holds that expire?

1137
01:17:52.166 --> 01:17:53.531
Thirty-second answer:

1138
01:17:53.811 --> 01:18:04.073
Try-Confirm-Cancel: try reserves resources, confirm makes them final, cancel releases them, and an expiry releases unconfirmed tries automatically.

1139
01:18:04.353 --> 01:18:16.321
To prevent overselling, confirm and expiry must be conditional updates in the same database: confirm only if the hold is still HELD and not expired, and release only if it's still HELD.

1140
01:18:17.671 --> 01:18:18.865
Question 14.

1141
01:18:19.145 --> 01:18:22.516
How do you guarantee ordering of events per order with an outbox?

1142
01:18:22.796 --> 01:18:24.161
Thirty-second answer:

1143
01:18:24.441 --> 01:18:30.265
Publish with the order id as the partition key, so Kafka keeps per-key order (Ch 14).

1144
01:18:30.545 --> 01:18:39.335
Run one active relay per shard, or partition relays by aggregate hash, so batches for the same aggregate aren't published concurrently.

1145
01:18:39.615 --> 01:18:45.311
Carry a per-aggregate sequence or version, so consumers can reject stale or duplicate events.

1146
01:18:46.661 --> 01:18:47.557
Architect.

1147
01:18:47.837 --> 01:18:49.031
Question 15.

1148
01:18:49.311 --> 01:18:51.146
When is 2PC the right choice?

1149
01:18:51.426 --> 01:18:52.791
Thirty-second answer:

1150
01:18:53.071 --> 01:19:04.869
When all participants are resource managers you control, in one failure domain, with a replicated or highly available coordinator, and blocking during coordinator recovery is acceptable.

1151
01:19:05.149 --> 01:19:13.063
For example, the multi-shard transactions inside a distributed SQL database, which runs 2PC over Raft groups.

1152
01:19:13.343 --> 01:19:16.586
Not across microservices or external APIs.

1153
01:19:17.936 --> 01:19:19.131
Question 16.

1154
01:19:19.411 --> 01:19:21.565
How do you test a saga implementation?

1155
01:19:21.845 --> 01:19:23.211
Thirty-second answer:

1156
01:19:23.491 --> 01:19:37.485
Inject a crash after every step (effect done, state not saved), inject timeouts with both didn't happen and happened outcomes, run many concurrent sagas, resume with the recovery path, then check cross-service invariants.

1157
01:19:37.765 --> 01:19:42.757
And run a negative control that removes idempotency, to prove the test can fail.

1158
01:19:44.107 --> 01:19:45.451
Question 17.

1159
01:19:45.731 --> 01:19:50.937
Would you build your own saga orchestrator or use Temporal or Camunda or Step Functions?

1160
01:19:51.217 --> 01:19:52.582
Thirty-second answer:

1161
01:19:52.862 --> 01:19:58.067
Build a small persisted state machine for one or two critical sagas you understand well.

1162
01:19:58.347 --> 01:20:01.761
It's a few hundred lines, observable and testable (L3).

1163
01:20:02.041 --> 01:20:14.030
Adopt a durable execution engine when you have many workflows, timers, human steps, versioning needs and long durations, where its retries, timers and history pay for the platform cost.

1164
01:20:15.380 --> 01:20:16.553
Question 18.

1165
01:20:16.833 --> 01:20:19.585
Moving money between two wallet shards: how?

1166
01:20:19.865 --> 01:20:21.231
Thirty-second answer:

1167
01:20:21.511 --> 01:20:27.228
Prefer avoiding it: co-locate a customer's ledger entries on one shard (Ch 11).

1168
01:20:27.508 --> 01:20:39.903
When it's unavoidable, use a transfer saga with a transfer record: debit shard A with a pending transfer entry (idempotent by transfer id), credit shard B (idempotent), then mark complete.

1169
01:20:40.183 --> 01:20:45.687
Reverse the debit if the credit can't happen, and reconcile both shards against the transfer table.

1170
01:20:47.037 --> 01:20:48.274
Question 19.

1171
01:20:48.554 --> 01:20:51.818
Your outbox table has 40 million unpublished rows.

1172
01:20:52.098 --> 01:20:53.869
What happened, and what do you do?

1173
01:20:54.149 --> 01:20:55.514
Thirty-second answer:

1174
01:20:55.794 --> 01:21:01.469
The relay stopped (crash loop, lost leadership, broker outage, a poison message) or can't keep up.

1175
01:21:01.749 --> 01:21:09.450
Stabilize first: fix or restart the relay, check the broker, and skip or park poison messages to a DLQ table.

1176
01:21:09.730 --> 01:21:15.703
Then drain with larger batches and more partition-parallel relays, protecting per-aggregate order.

1177
01:21:15.983 --> 01:21:18.394
Then add backlog-age alerting and cleanup.

1178
01:21:19.744 --> 01:21:20.853
Question 20.

1179
01:21:21.133 --> 01:21:25.656
How do you know your distributed transactions are actually consistent in production?

1180
01:21:25.936 --> 01:21:27.301
Thirty-second answer:

1181
01:21:27.581 --> 01:21:29.331
Continuous reconciliation.

1182
01:21:29.611 --> 01:21:43.307
Scheduled jobs check cross-service invariants: confirmed orders ⇔ captured payments ⇔ confirmed reservations, stock conservation, ledger sums to zero, internal payments versus the Razorpay settlement report.

1183
01:21:43.587 --> 01:21:48.173
Mismatches are alerted and worked, and every mismatch class leads to a fix.

1184
01:21:49.523 --> 01:21:50.419
Part 9.

1185
01:21:50.699 --> 01:21:52.107
Summary and Practice.

1186
01:21:53.357 --> 01:21:54.445
Key takeaways.

1187
01:21:54.725 --> 01:22:02.576
A local transaction can't span services. @Transactional around remote calls is the root of charged-without-order bugs.

1188
01:22:02.856 --> 01:22:05.523
Dual writes lose or invent events (L1:

1189
01:22:05.803 --> 01:22:10.688
1,696 lost, 1,118 phantom).

1190
01:22:10.968 --> 01:22:14.125
The outbox makes the event part of the local transaction.

1191
01:22:14.405 --> 01:22:16.069
Delivery is at least once.

1192
01:22:16.349 --> 01:22:22.749
Make effects exactly once with an inbox or natural idempotency in the same transaction (L2:

1193
01:22:23.029 --> 01:22:25.867
2,403, then 2,000).

1194
01:22:26.147 --> 01:22:33.336
Relay parallelism can reorder: use one relay per shard or partition, key by aggregate, and carry versions.

1195
01:22:34.686 --> 01:22:44.969
Sagas = local steps + compensations, orchestrated for complex flows, with persisted, conditionally updated state and a recovery worker (L3:

1196
01:22:45.249 --> 01:22:47.211
310 crashes resumed).

1197
01:22:47.491 --> 01:22:52.227
Every step is idempotent by saga id, and timeouts are unknown outcomes.

1198
01:22:52.507 --> 01:22:57.265
Retry with the same key and never compensate blindly (negative control:

1199
01:22:57.545 --> 01:22:59.657
141 double charges).

1200
01:22:59.937 --> 01:23:01.601
Sagas have no isolation.

1201
01:23:01.881 --> 01:23:06.382
Use semantic locks, commutative updates and product decisions (L3:

1202
01:23:06.662 --> 01:23:10.161
115 rejected while 21 were unsold).

1203
01:23:11.511 --> 01:23:20.087
TCC holds need expiry and guarded confirms in the same DB statement (L4: unguarded oversold 301).

1204
01:23:20.367 --> 01:23:25.273
2PC blocks when the coordinator fails, even across restarts (lab 1).

1205
01:23:25.553 --> 01:23:27.196
Keep it inside one system.

1206
01:23:27.476 --> 01:23:35.540
CDC gives committed changes in commit order, but slots retain WAL and need heartbeats and monitoring (lab 2).

